  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Today will be unseasonably warm with high \ntemperatures that are 15 degrees above normal.\n\nHigh pressure to our south and a cold front approaching from the\nnorthwest will funnel warm temperatures into the region by way\nof southwesterly winds. Strong warm air advection will result in\nan afternoon T-max that is about 15 degrees above the seasonal\nnorm with readings near 90 for the foothills and piedmont, and\nupper 70s to mid 80s for the mountains and mountain valleys\nalong and west of the Blue Ridge. Similar to yesterday could see\na few wind gusts near 25 mph during the early afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Expect much cooler temperatures with overcast \nconditions this weekend with opportunity for rain.\n\nBroad upper level trough over the northern U.S. to swing through\nthe region this weekend. The cold front associated with the\nleading edge of this airmass will cross the forecast area\ntonight. Showers ahead of the front are expected to move across\nWest Virginia this afternoon to along the VA/WV border by\nsunset. Front will then cross VA and into NC during the\novernight with potential for showers and a slight chance of\nthunderstorms. Models bring surge of much cooler air into the\nregion during the late night hours and will set the stage for a\ncool damp fall weekend...temperatures in the 60s for highs. \nModels are now indicating the frontal showers may weaken enough \non Saturday that some areas, especially VA/NC border counties, \nsee little or no rain Saturday. However, by Sunday, a wave of \nlow pressure is expected to develop along the front with \npotential for over-running rain to spread back across the region\nSaturday night and Sunday with a better opportunity for wetting\nrain across the VA/NC border counties for the second half of \nthe weekend. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Crisp fall weather next week with cool temperatures and \nanother stretch of dry days.\n\nClearing is expected from north to south Monday as the weekend\nfront moves farther south and away from the area. High pressure\nwill build into the area from the north providing for a string\nof cool dry fall weather days next week. \n\nTemperatures through the week are expected to be near normal\nwith highs of 65 to 75 and lows in the 40s. There may be a night\nor two where lows slip into the 30s in the mountain valleys.",
